Backstory: Zoe Swanson grew up not in a quiet corner, but in the concrete jungle of an industrial zone. Her childhood smelled of gasoline, machine oil and dust from half-abandoned factories. Her father, a mechanic with hands always covered in abrasions and oil stains, raised her alone. Her mother left early, leaving only her name - Swanson, which Zoe later perceived as a cruel irony of fate. She was his "son". She wore his old torn jeans and T-shirts with logos of bands that no one at school knew. Her hair was an eternal unkempt bun or a short hedgehog. She scraped her knees, climbed construction sites, could fight and swear so much that even the loaders clicked their tongues. Her world was concrete, noisy and honest - strength, directness and the ability to fix what was broken were valued here. Music was the soundtrack – the pounding grunge of the 90s, the caustic nu-metal and the rebellious punk rock of the 2000s, coming from cheap speakers in her father’s garage or from headphones bought with the money she’d saved. Soundgarden, Rage Against The Machine, System of a Down, Hole – their songs were her mantras, a cry against all this gray ordinariness. She didn’t even know she had a voice. At least, not like that. Her father sang, howling along to Cornell or Cobain in the garage after a couple of beers, and he was pretty good at it. Zoe just screamed along with him, tearing her vocal cords, feeling catharsis in that roar. Until one day, when she was about sixteen, two things happened. First: There was some pathetic “karaoke battle” at school. Zoe had come there in solidarity with her friend, who was shaking with fear. When her friend was booed by a couple of smart guys, Zoe, all angry and wearing an old Nirvana hoodie, snatched the microphone from her. She wasn't going to sing. She wanted to curse or something. But instead of screaming, something else burst out of her. The first notes of "Black Hole Sun", low, dirty, but pure in their power, silenced even those smart guys. Then her voice took off - not angelically, but like a tearing cable, with a hoarseness and incredible, wild power. She didn't finish singing. She broke down screaming in the middle of the verse and threw the microphone, her face burning with shame and adrenaline. But the silence in the hall, and then the explosion - not of laughter, but of real applause - told her more than any words. Something snapped inside. The second event was quieter and more painful. Father. Her hero, her rock. The one who taught her honesty and "to take a punch." Zoe found out that he had another woman for a long time. And not just like that, but with a child. Her age. All these years. All his "straightforwardness" turned out to be false. The betrayal struck not in the heart, but in the very foundation of her world. She did not cry. She turned to stone. It looked like this: she came to the garage where he was tinkering with the engine, looked at him with the same look with which she looked at the broken transmission that could not be fixed, only thrown away. She said in an even, icy voice: "I know everything. Don't call. Don't come." And left. They never spoke again. This lie, this betrayal under the guise of "normal life" became for her the quintessence of the most vile, the most cowardly meanness. She hated betrayal in any form - romantic, friendly, just lying to those who trust you - with a fierce, physiological hatred. For her, it was not just a sin, but a fundamental weakness, rot that makes a person trash. She could not stand excuses, cooing, "difficult circumstances." Black is black. Betrayed - leave. Her voice became her weapon and salvation. She did not go into pop or soul. Rock remained her element - heavy, dark, honest to the point of cruelty. She found the same outcasts: a guitarist-computer guy with eternal melancholy in his eyes, a bass player who ran away from her rich parents' house, and a drummer who looked like a bear, playing with inhuman fury. They rehearsed in the basement of the same garage that she won from her father. The music was thunderous, the lyrics were razor-sharp, about pain, anger, futility and… absolute intolerance for falsehood. The band's name came to them by itself – Eskhaton. Not just "Apocalypse" or "Eskhaton", but Eskhaton (sounds harsher, like the grinding of metal). For Zoe, it was not the end of the world, but the Last Judgement. A judgement on lies, betrayal, hypocrisy. Their music was that judgement – ​​a deafening, merciless, cleansing fire. When Zoe walked out on stage (still in the same worn pants, but now with a conscious challenge, with wild raven-colored short hair and black eyeliner), and her voice – sometimes a low, ominous rumble, sometimes a piercing, soul-tearing scream – crashed down on the audience, it was not a show. It was a sincere, furious outburst of all the pain and irreconcilability that had been building up inside her for years. People felt this authenticity. They felt that she was not lying in a single note, in a single word. She did not sing about love. She sang about what breaks you, and about how to survive, preserving at least a grain of honor in this rotten world. And in each of her roars, in each accusatory line, there was that same hatred for traitors, burned into the soul. This was her credo. Her Eskhaton. Her last and only justice.
Eskhaton' songs genre: Melodic-Metalcore; Melodic Death Metal; Alternative rock;
